    },
    {
      "topic": "position_size",
      "choice": "size=0.1 passed directly to buy()",
      "reason": "'Lot' has no direct backtrader/equity analog; the spec's own example value (0.1 lot) is used literally as the order size parameter."
    },
    {
      "topic": "entry_signal",
      "choice": "Enter long at market whenever flat; no short entries are triggered, though short-side SL/TP/trailing mechanics are fully implemented and would apply if a short were ever opened",
      "reason": "The spec describes long and short position management symmetrically but never states a signal, pattern, or condition that determines direction or timing of entry ('price action' is mentioned but not defined). Per canonical default (long-only unless spec explicitly supplies a shorting trigger) and to avoid inventing an unstated entry pattern, only long entries are generated on this feed."
    },
    {
      "topic": "infeasible",
      "choice": "Stop-loss/take-profit are managed as internal price levels checked against each bar's High/Low in next(), closing via self.close() when breached, rather than as live broker Stop/Limit orders that are cancelled and resubmitted",
      "reason": "Backtrader has no true order-modify primitive and no analog to broker 'freeze level'/minimum-distance requirements; the closest literal implementation of 'before modifying an order, make sure the price allows it' is the monotonic ratchet (stop-loss only ever moves toward/into profit, never loosens), which is enforced directly in the level-update logic."
    },
    {
      "topic": "same_bar_sl_tp_conflict",
      "choice": "If both stop-loss and take-profit levels are touched within the same bar, stop-loss is checked first and takes precedence",
      "reason": "Spec does not address simultaneous same-bar triggering; the stop-loss-first ordering is the more conservative reading."
    },
    {
      "topic": "profit_calc_price_source",
      "choice": "Use each bar's favorable intrabar extreme (High for longs, Low for shorts) both to detect stop-loss/take-profit breaches and to compute the profit tier driving the trailing-stop ratchet",
      "reason": "The spec says the stop moves 'when profit reaches' a given level, implying the price actually touched that level; with only OHLC bars, the intrabar High/Low is the closest literal proxy for that contact."
    }
